WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.028 --> 00:00:01.140
(Clapping)

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:02.600
- So I already love you, I mean
we all have siblings right?

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:04.260
And that's our relationship
with all of them, but I bet

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.878
you all have some pretty
good brother stories.

00:00:06.878 --> 00:00:07.711
- Yeah we do.

00:00:07.711 --> 00:00:09.521
So, there's five of us
and we're all brothers.

00:00:09.521 --> 00:00:10.530
- Five? There's five boys?

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:11.363
- [John] Yes.

00:00:11.363 --> 00:00:13.034
- Your poor mother.

00:00:13.034 --> 00:00:13.867
(Laughing)

00:00:13.867 --> 00:00:15.820
- Yeah, yeah, there's
no other way to say it.

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:16.653
Yup, my poor mom.

00:00:16.653 --> 00:00:17.486
- Wow.

00:00:17.486 --> 00:00:20.310
I mean, and lucky her, but I
just mean that's a lot of boys.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.180
- Yeah, and we were, how's
the best way for me...

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:24.310
We weren't good kids.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:25.890
- [Dan] Aggressive, very aggressive.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.850
- Yes we were, there might have been a few

00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:32.170
police stops at our house growing up.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:33.970
- He's like, "I gotta be quiet now".

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.090
- You called him out,
he's now calling you out.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.350
- Yeah, we both spent quite a bit of our

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:40.970
youth and adolescense
running from the law.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.420
And one of us was like,
"Man I can out do these guys

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:45.180
so I'm just gonna get involved".

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.013
- He ran to them.

00:00:46.013 --> 00:00:46.846
- Yeah.

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:47.679
- He done ran to them.

00:00:47.679 --> 00:00:48.570
Any stories on Dan?

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.790
Like possibly rookie moves
that he thinks he doesn't have

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.460
but he did make some rookie moves?

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.030
- Ya know, I'm certainly not gonna put him

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:56.790
on the spot in that way.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:00:58.480
I'm gonna put him on the
spot in a different way.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:00.650
And many times when we
get together as a family,

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.180
Dan's the only one
involved in law enforcement

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.500
and first responding.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.323
And in the few times that
we've had a chance to talk,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.240
he's been able to relay to
us that no matter how hard

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.360
we try, he's just seen
and done things that

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:18.383
we'll never be able to actualize.

00:01:21.044 --> 00:01:25.820
It makes me feel distant at
points, but at the same time

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:29.320
I love and respect the fact
that he is so dedicated

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.240
to his job and his purpose.

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.590
That he's honest enough to be
like, "Hey I know you're gonna

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:38.130
try to relate to me on this
level, but what I've seen,

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.650
you have no idea what
I go through every day.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:43.530
And I just wanna be able to lean on

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.920
my brothers and sisters
in arms to do that".

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.310
So that's kinda--

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.379
- Okay, so I'm crying, it's fine.

00:01:50.379 --> 00:01:52.230
- That's a difficult speech
for us but it gives me...

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:56.600
I texted him the other day,
we have a very... we grew

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.610
up in a man's man house, and its not,

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:01.790
we don't show emotion
that well and all that.

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:03.649
And I've had to learn to
unhardwire those things.

00:02:03.649 --> 00:02:04.482
- You're doing a great job.

00:02:04.482 --> 00:02:06.255
- Thank you, thank you.

00:02:06.255 --> 00:02:10.820
- One thing I truly love
about Dan is he, as much

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:15.200
of the job that is difficult
to digest, he loves his work

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.280
and he never once takes it home with him.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.190
And he's a wonderful father,
and a wonderful husband, and

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:21.080
a wonderful brother.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.350
And to be able to manage
all that, I can't even

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.600
manage my own life, and for him to be able

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:26.700
to manage all that

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.810
- [Kelly] Wow.

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.038
- Is super cool.

00:02:29.038 --> 00:02:29.871
[Clapping]

00:02:29.871 --> 00:02:31.503
- That is so nice.

00:02:31.503 --> 00:02:32.810
[Cheers]

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.327
- [Kelly] That was so well said.

00:02:34.327 --> 00:02:37.280
- I'm very, very grateful
that you asked him to be here.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:38.747
And literally when your
producers were like,

00:02:38.747 --> 00:02:40.250
"We're thinking about maybe
getting your brother Dan".

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.110
And I came up with the bit
making fun of me by the way,

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.920
because I told him just to go to town.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.120
But I didn't think he would
go, because he's often so

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:49.359
dedicated to his
department and to his work.

00:02:49.359 --> 00:02:50.192
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:50.192 --> 00:02:52.470
- And I think its awesome
that, in meeting a lot of

00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.070
first responders, in doing this
film that we got coming up,

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.248
were gonna talk about it later.

00:02:56.248 --> 00:02:58.065
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:58.065 --> 00:03:00.490
- But every so often, whenever
you get to talk to them,

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.490
they want their department and they want

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:05.320
their unit showcased, but they never care

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.050
to be like, "Hey I did
this" so good on my brother

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:11.810
for doing the bit, but
he's always department

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.690
and unit first above all else.

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.350
I think that is because,
and forgive me if I'm

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:19.960
speaking out of terms, but in
difficult times, especially

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:23.420
in subject matter that we
can't relate to, you folks

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.300
lean on each other.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:25.530
And I think that's very special.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:26.950
You have your family,

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:28.055
- [Kelly] A bond.

00:03:28.055 --> 00:03:29.413
- and then you have your family.

00:03:29.413 --> 00:03:30.246
- Yeah.

00:03:30.246 --> 00:03:31.079
- Which is really, really special.

00:03:31.079 --> 00:03:32.387
- It's so, so nice.

00:03:32.387 --> 00:03:34.078
(Clapping)

00:03:34.078 --> 00:03:36.600
- But Dan, actually what's interesting is,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.980
you're not only a first
responder, your life has been

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.240
saved by a first responder.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:41.073
- [John] True.

00:03:41.073 --> 00:03:42.178
- Yep.

00:03:42.178 --> 00:03:43.011
- [Kelly] What happened?

00:03:43.011 --> 00:03:46.163
- Well, back home I worked
for a few different agencies.

00:03:47.388 --> 00:03:51.142
And when I was employed for the
town of Newberry, I was just

00:03:51.142 --> 00:03:53.850
working after Super Bowl
Sunday, back in 2007.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.840
And I just took a right
onto Route 1 and I started

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.470
heading northbound in my cruiser.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.320
I reached 25 miles an
hour and next thing I know

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:03.970
I get hit head on at 76 miles
an hour by a fourth time

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.889
drunk driver it took me--

00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:06.722
- A fourth time?

00:04:06.722 --> 00:04:09.255
- It took me out of the
game for a little while.

00:04:09.255 --> 00:04:10.500
That's the picture of what's left of...

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.826
that cruiser had about 2,500 miles on it

00:04:12.826 --> 00:04:13.950
when I was driving it.

00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:15.193
- Wow.

00:04:15.193 --> 00:04:16.026
- So yeah, it got banged up pretty bad.

00:04:16.026 --> 00:04:19.020
I was very lucky to get
out of there in whatever

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:19.853
shape I'm in now.

00:04:19.853 --> 00:04:22.940
It only broke my left leg,
which is pretty amazing

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:24.920
looking at the devastation
done to that car.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.100
And you think it could
have been a lot worse,

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.170
if not life ending.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:29.900
- I'm glad it was only your
leg though, you're right

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.620
you got lucky because I saw the car.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.166
- [Dan] Yeah.

00:04:33.166 --> 00:04:34.030
- So that is very lucky,
but you were saved

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:35.920
by first responders.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:36.830
- Absolutely, yeah they were
right down the road from me

00:04:36.830 --> 00:04:38.640
and were able to get me
out of my car that was...

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.510
I couldn't open the door because
it was crushed in so bad.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:42.890
So one of the junior guys
actually came running up to me

00:04:42.890 --> 00:04:44.150
and ripped the door open.

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.740
I went to go put pressure
on my leg to get out,

00:04:45.740 --> 00:04:46.573
it just collapsed.

00:04:46.573 --> 00:04:47.410
And I said I think I broke my leg.

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:49.487
And he dragged me out, and I broke my leg.

00:04:49.487 --> 00:04:50.513
[Kelly] Wow.

